  "facts": {
    "point": "§FS-check.1.1.9",
    "authoredFrom": "An escaped ID is not counted as a use (§FS-citations.recognized.escapes, §FS-check.4.1). Read from the case's input bytes: grund.toml (version 1, marker §, `strict = false`, `[id] format = \"{kind}-{slug}\"` with slug pattern `[a-z][a-z0-9-]*`, default kinds and scan), FS-login.md and FS-session.md (each a heading and a one-line body, lines 2-3), the FS index with both linked entries on lines 5 and 6, and docs/guide.md. guide.md line 3's bare FS-login is a citation in this compatibility mode (§FS-citations.recognized). Lines 6 and 7 write `<§>FS-login` and `<§>FS-session`: a bare ID that begins right after the escape (the configured marker in angle brackets) is no citation in either strict mode, and the exclusion is total, so neither appears in `refs`, `cover` or the editor's citations nor counts as a use (§FS-citations.recognized.escapes, §FS-check.1.1.9). Their `escaped-citation-resolves` suggestions (§FS-check.2.3.1) are withheld from every run without --suggestions and are not pushed by the editor (§FS-check.2.3.2, §FS-lsp.1.1). FS-login is therefore cited once by guide.md and once by its index entry; FS-session only by its index entry, which is no use (§FS-check.4.1.2), so it is unused at its heading with the appended index-entry explanation naming README.md:6 (§FS-check.4.1.2). The version caution is site-less and sorts first (§FS-check.2.1.1, §FS-errors.4.1). `refs` lists every index entry, which is a real citation (§FS-check.4.1.2, §FS-refs.2). Fmt adds no marker without --marker (§FS-fmt.2.2), never rewrites an escape position (§FS-fmt.2.3), and the index's marked entries already link where the cross-reference writer would (§FS-fmt.6.3). FS-404 is no ID under this slug pattern, so FS-absent is the absent control.",
